Sweep line: split each booking into a start and an end event, sort them, and walk through counting. A start adds a room, an end frees one. Because a booking ending exactly when another starts can share the room, process ends before starts at the same timestamp. The peak count is the answer.

```js
function minRooms(bookings) {
  const events = [];
  for (const [start, end] of bookings) {
    events.push([start, 1], [end, -1]);
  }
  // ends (-1) before starts (+1) at the same time
  events.sort((a, b) => a[0] - b[0] || a[1] - b[1]);

  let rooms = 0, peak = 0;
  for (const [, delta] of events) {
    rooms += delta;
    peak = Math.max(peak, rooms);
  }
  return peak;
}
```

O(n log n) time, O(n) space. The alternative is sorting starts and ends separately and merging with two pointers, which is also O(n log n) and O(n).
